# Example environment file.
# Copy this to ".env" and fill in values only if you need them.
# For normal local development you do NOT need any of these — the app
# runs on a local SQLite database with zero setup.

# --- Database ---
# Leave this UNSET to use the local SQLite database (data/local.db).
# Set it to a PostgreSQL connection string to use PostgreSQL instead,
# then run `npm run db:push` once to create the tables.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/onpoint

# --- Sessions ---
# A secret used to sign login cookies. Required in production — the server
# refuses to start without one. Left unset in development, a fresh random one
# is generated on every start; nothing is lost by that, because sessions there
# are held in memory and already end when the server restarts.
# SESSION_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# --- The first teacher account ---
# Used ONLY when seeding a brand new, empty database. An account that already
# exists keeps the password it has — change that one with:
#   npm run set-teacher-password
#
# Left unset: development uses a known throwaway value so the check scripts can
# sign in, and anywhere else gets a random one, printed once at startup for
# whoever is setting the school up.
# SEED_TEACHER_PASSWORD=

# --- Signing in as a pupil ---
# Lets staff open a child's account without asking for their password, by
# typing this instead of the child's own password on the student login.
#
# Leave it UNSET and the feature is simply off, which is the safe default.
# There is no built-in value: the one that used to be in the source code is
# published in this repository's history and is refused outright.
#
# If you want it, generate one and keep it somewhere only staff can read:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('base64url'))"
# MASTER_PASSWORD=

# --- Server ---
# Port the app listens on (defaults to 5000).
# PORT=5000

# --- Android app (Play Store) ---
# Only needed once the app is packaged for Android. These two make the server
# publish /.well-known/assetlinks.json, which is how Android confirms the app and
# this website have the same owner — that is what removes the browser address bar
# from the top of the app. See docs/ANDROID_PACKAGING.md.
#
# The app's package (application) ID:
# ANDROID_PACKAGE_NAME=zw.co.onpointeducation.homework
#
# The SHA-256 signing fingerprint(s) from Play Console. Separate several with
# commas — you normally need BOTH the Play "app signing" key and the
# "upload" key so the app works before and after Google re-signs it.
# ANDROID_CERT_FINGERPRINTS=AB:CD:...:EF,12:34:...:56
